## File Tree & Expansion Patterns

### 🚨 Chevron Expansion Flakiness

**Individual directory expansion via chevrons may be flaky under test due to timing/virtualization.** If you can’t stabilize with better locators and assertions, this workaround is acceptable:

#### ✅ Stable Workaround

```ts
// 1) Filter for the nested file
const filterInput = page.getByPlaceholder("Type to filter files... (e.g. 'test component -spec')");
await filterInput.fill('Button');

// 2) Use "Expand All"
await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Expand All' }).click();

// 3) Assert nested file is visible
await expect(page.getByText('Button.tsx')).toBeVisible();

// 4) Optionally clear the filter
await filterInput.fill('');
```

#### Prefer a More Semantic Approach First

If your tree implements ARIA roles, target rows and expansion **state**:

```ts
const folder = page.getByRole('treeitem', { name: 'src/components' });
await folder.getByRole('button', { name: /expand/i }).click();

// Assert expansion via aria-expanded
await expect(folder).toHaveAttribute('aria-expanded', 'true');
```

> Leveraging role/state (e.g., `treeitem`, `aria-expanded`) plus web-first assertions is the recommended direction before falling back to global "expand all".

